Week 2 — Security Planning & Testing Methodology
← Week 1 | Week 2 | Week 3 →
Overview
Week 2 focuses on designing a security baseline and defining a structured performance testing methodology before implementing any security controls. This planning-first approach ensures that security configurations are measurable, repeatable, and aligned with real-world operating system security practices.
The goal of this phase is to identify realistic threats, define mitigation strategies, and establish how system performance and security effectiveness will be evaluated throughout the coursework.
Objectives
The objectives for Week 2 are to:
Design a comprehensive operating system security baseline
Identify realistic security threats relevant to a Linux server
Define mitigation strategies for identified threats
Plan a structured performance testing methodology
Define metrics, tools, and evidence collection strategies
Performance Testing Plan
To evaluate operating system behaviour under different workloads, performance testing will be conducted remotely from the workstation using SSH-based monitoring tools. Testing will be performed in a controlled and repeatable manner to ensure reliable results.
Testing Approach
Performance testing will be carried out in the following stages:
Baseline Testing
Measure system performance when the server is idle with only essential services running.
Application Load Testing
Measure resource usage while running selected workload applications.
Bottleneck Identification
Analyse collected data to identify CPU, memory, disk I/O, or network constraints.
Optimisation Testing
Apply configuration or tuning changes and re-test to quantify improvements.
Performance Metrics
The following metrics will be monitored where appropriate:
CPU utilisation (%)
Memory usage (used, free, cache)
Disk usage and I/O throughput
Network latency and throughput
System load averages
Service response times
Monitoring Tools
Performance data will be collected using standard Linux command-line utilities executed remotely via SSH:
top / htop — CPU and process monitoring
free — memory usage
df and iostat — disk usage and I/O performance
ip, ss, ping — network monitoring
Custom monitoring scripts (developed in later weeks)
All monitoring will be performed without installing a graphical interface on the server.
Security Configuration Checklist
The following checklist defines the mandatory security controls to be implemented in later phases of the coursework:
SSH Security
Disable password-based authentication
Enable key-based authentication
Restrict SSH access to a single trusted workstation
Change default SSH configuration where appropriate
Disable root SSH login
Firewall Configuration
Enable host-based firewall (UFW)
Allow SSH traffic only from the workstation IP
Deny all other inbound connections by default
Verify firewall rules after configuration
Mandatory Access Control
Enable and configure AppArmor (or SELinux)
Verify enforcement mode is active
Monitor and log access control violations
Automatic Updates
Enable unattended security updates
Verify update service is running and scheduled
Ensure minimal disruption to system availability
User Privilege Management
Create a non-root administrative user
Enforce least-privilege access using sudo
Remove unnecessary default users
Audit user and group permissions
Network Security
Minimise exposed network services
Verify listening ports and services
Ensure unnecessary services are disabled
Threat Model
A threat model was developed to identify realistic risks to the Linux server and define appropriate mitigation strategies.
Threat 1: Brute-Force SSH Attacks
Description: Attackers attempt to gain access by guessing SSH credentials.
Impact: Unauthorised system access and potential data compromise.
Mitigation:
Disable password authentication
Use SSH key-based authentication
Restrict SSH access by IP
Deploy fail2ban for intrusion detection
Threat 2: Exploitation of Vulnerable Services
Description: Outdated or unnecessary services may contain exploitable vulnerabilities.
Impact: Remote code execution or privilege escalation.
Mitigation:
Enable automatic security updates
Remove unnecessary services
Regularly audit running services
Use firewall rules to restrict access
Threat 3: Misuse of Excessive Privileges
Description: Users with unnecessary administrative privileges increase risk.
Impact: Accidental or malicious system misconfiguration.
Mitigation:
Enforce least-privilege principles
Use sudo for administrative tasks
Monitor authentication and privilege escalation logs
Evidence and Documentation Strategy
To support later analysis and assessment requirements:
All configuration changes will be documented with before-and-after comparisons
Screenshots will include visible terminal prompts (username@hostname)
Logs and command outputs will be captured and referenced
Performance data will be recorded in structured tables
Graphs and charts will be generated from collected data
Learning Outcome Alignment
LO3: Addressed through threat modelling and planned implementation of security controls to mitigate operating system vulnerabilities.
LO4: Demonstrated through planned use of command-line tools for monitoring, auditing, and configuration.
LO5: Addressed by evaluating security versus performance trade-offs introduced by hardening measures.
Reflection (Week 2)
This week emphasised the importance of planning security and performance evaluation before system configuration. Developing a structured threat model and security checklist ensures that security controls are not applied arbitrarily but are instead driven by identified risks.
Additionally, defining a clear performance testing methodology establishes a baseline against which the impact of security hardening and optimisation can be quantitatively measured in later weeks.
